// Rep Max questions: History (OCR J411 BF), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"ghi-001","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":1,"q":"Many medieval people believed disease was spread by 'miasma'. What was miasma?","o":["Tiny living things in water","A punishment sent by the king","Poison put in wells by foreigners","Foul-smelling air from rotting matter"],"a":3,"x":"Medieval people thought bad smells carried disease. Germ theory was not developed until the 1860s, so towns tried to remove smells from rubbish, sewage and slaughterhouses.","w":["Mixes it up with germ theory, which only came in the 1860s.","Confuses it with 'disease as punishment'; miasma was about bad air.","Mixes it up with well-poisoning rumours in the Black Death; miasma was smelly air.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-002","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":2,"q":"During the Black Death in 1348-49, what did flagellants do?","o":["Shut sick people inside their homes","Burned the clothes of the dead","Whipped themselves in public to ask God to end the plague","Left towns to live in the countryside"],"a":2,"x":"Flagellants believed the plague was God's punishment and that suffering publicly would win forgiveness. Their processions spread from Europe, and they sometimes spread disease.","w":["That was quarantine in later plagues, not flagellants' penance.","Burning clothes was a precaution, not a religious act of penance.","","Fleeing to the countryside was what the rich did; flagellants walked between towns."]}
{"id":"ghi-003","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":1,"q":"During plague outbreaks in the 1600s, what happened to a house where someone had plague?","o":["It was burned down straight away","It was shut up with the family inside and marked with a red cross","It was given to the church","The family was moved to the countryside"],"a":1,"x":"Houses were shut up for forty days with a red cross on the door, guarded by watchmen. This protected others but was cruel to healthy people shut in with the sick.","w":["Infected houses were sealed and guarded, not burned down.","","No rule gave plague houses to the church.","Wealthy people fled; infected families were kept shut in."]}
{"id":"ghi-004","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":2,"q":"Why was the 1751 Gin Act more effective than the 1736 Gin Act?","o":["It stopped distillers selling to unlicensed sellers","It banned the making of all alcohol","It made gin free for the poor","It was enforced by the army"],"a":0,"x":"The 1736 Act's £50 licence was so high that only two were ever bought, so the trade went underground. The 1751 Act banned distillers selling to unlicensed shops, which could be enforced. Rising grain prices also cut gin drinking.","w":["","The Act only targeted unlicensed gin sales, not all alcohol.","The aim was to cut gin drinking, not give it away free.","Enforcement relied on magistrates and licensing, not the army."]}
{"id":"ghi-005","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":1,"q":"In 1854 John Snow linked a cholera outbreak in Soho to:","o":["A bad smell from the Thames","A shipment of foreign cotton","A water pump in Broad Street","Overcrowding in lodging houses"],"a":2,"x":"Snow mapped the deaths and found they clustered around the Broad Street pump. The handle was removed, though cases were already falling. His evidence supported his idea that cholera spread through dirty water.","w":["Mixes up miasma ideas with Snow's evidence; he blamed water, not smells.","Cotton had no link to the Soho cholera outbreak.","","Overcrowding was a real problem, but Snow's map pointed to one pump."]}
{"id":"ghi-006","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":2,"q":"What was a main weakness of the 1848 Public Health Act?","o":["Most of its measures were not compulsory for local councils","It only applied to London","It banned all new factories","It set up no Board of Health"],"a":0,"x":"The 1848 Act created a General Board of Health but local councils were mostly not forced to act unless the death rate was very high. Many ignored it because reform cost money.","w":["","It actually left London out; London was covered by separate laws.","It didn't ban factories; it was about water, sewers and health.","It did set up a General Board of Health."]}
{"id":"ghi-007","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":1,"q":"Which Act of 1956 followed the Great Smog of London in 1952?","o":["The Public Health Act","The Housing Act","The National Health Service Act","The Clean Air Act"],"a":3,"x":"The Great Smog of December 1952 was linked to thousands of deaths. The Clean Air Act 1956 created smokeless zones and reduced coal smoke in cities.","w":["Public Health Acts were 1848 and 1875, long before the 1952 smog.","The Housing Act dealt with slums, not smoke in the air.","The NHS Act was 1946 and was about healthcare, not pollution.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-008","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":2,"q":"What was the main aim of the government's 'Don't Die of Ignorance' AIDS campaign in 1986-87?","o":["To announce a vaccine had been found","To tell people how AIDS is spread and how to reduce the risk","To ban people with AIDS from public places","To raise money for blood testing only"],"a":1,"x":"There was no cure, so the government used leaflets sent to every home and TV adverts to spread information about how HIV was passed on and how to avoid catching it.","w":["No vaccine existed; that is why the campaign focused on prevention.","","The campaign gave advice; it didn't ban anyone from public places.","It was an information campaign, not a fundraiser."]}
{"id":"ghi-009","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":1,"q":"The creation of the NHS in 1948 is the best example of which factor in changing health?","o":["Religious belief","Chance discovery","Government action","Natural disasters"],"a":2,"x":"The NHS, set up by Clement Attlee's Labour government with Aneurin Bevan as health minister, gave free healthcare for all. It shows the role of government in improving public health.","w":["The NHS was a political decision, not a religious one.","It was planned policy from the Beveridge Report, not luck.","","Disasters didn't create the NHS; a government did."]}
{"id":"ghi-010","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":3,"q":"Why was Louis Pasteur's germ theory of the 1860s important for public health?","o":["It proved that bad air causes disease","It gave scientific evidence that microbes cause disease","It produced a cure for cholera","It led directly to the NHS"],"a":1,"x":"Pasteur showed that microbes cause disease, replacing the belief in miasma. This gave governments and scientists a scientific reason to provide clean water and sewers.","w":["Pasteur's theory disproved miasma; it didn't support bad air.","","Pasteur didn't cure cholera; Koch identified its microbe in 1883.","The NHS came in 1948, far later and for other reasons."]}
{"id":"ghi-011","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":1,"q":"Who were the members of Elizabeth I's Privy Council?","o":["Advisers chosen by the queen to help her govern","MPs elected by landowners","Bishops chosen by the Pope","Judges who tried criminals"],"a":0,"x":"The Privy Council was a small group of nobles and officials, such as William Cecil, who advised Elizabeth and ran the government day to day. She chose its members.","w":["","That describes Parliament; the Privy Council was chosen by the queen.","Elizabeth was Supreme Governor of the Church; the Pope had no say.","Judges ran the courts; the Council advised on government."]}
{"id":"ghi-012","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":3,"q":"Why did the Earl of Essex rebel against Elizabeth in February 1601?","o":["He wanted to make England Catholic again","He had lost favour and wanted to remove her advisers and win back influence","He had been paid by Spain to kill the queen","He wanted to end taxes on the poor"],"a":1,"x":"Essex had failed in Ireland and lost royal favour, and the queen refused to renew his sweet wine monopoly, leaving him in debt. He wanted to remove rivals such as Robert Cecil. He rose on 8 February and was executed on 25 February 1601.","w":["Essex was Protestant; this was a bid for power, not a religious rebellion.","","No evidence Spain paid him; he wanted to remove rivals at court.","He acted for his own influence, not to help the poor."]}
{"id":"ghi-013","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":1,"q":"In which year was the Spanish Armada defeated?","o":["1558","1570","1588","1603"],"a":2,"x":"The Armada sailed in 1588 to invade England. English fireships, the weather and the Spanish being unable to join up with their army led to its defeat.","w":["Mixes it up with Elizabeth becoming queen in 1558, 30 years too early","1570 is when the Pope excommunicated Elizabeth, not the Armada","","1603 is the year Elizabeth died, long after the Armada"]}
{"id":"ghi-014","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":3,"q":"What was the result of the Babington Plot of 1586 for Mary, Queen of Scots?","o":["She was freed and allowed to leave England","She was crowned queen of England","Her letters were found and she was executed in 1587","She was sent to live in France"],"a":2,"x":"Walsingham's spies intercepted Mary's letters approving a plot to kill Elizabeth. She was tried for treason and executed at Fotheringhay in February 1587.","w":["Opposite: the plot ended any hope of freedom for Mary","Mary wanted the crown, but the plot failed and she never got it","","She lived in France as a child, but in 1586 she was a prisoner in England"]}
{"id":"ghi-015","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":1,"q":"In Elizabethan law, who were the 'impotent poor'?","o":["Healthy men who refused to work","People who could not work, such as the old, sick and disabled","Wandering players and actors","Landowners who could not pay taxes"],"a":1,"x":"The impotent poor were seen as 'deserving' because they could not work and were helped, for example in almshouses. Fit beggars, the 'sturdy beggars', could be punished.","w":["Confuses them with 'sturdy beggars', who were fit but idle and punished","","","'Impotent' means unable to work, not unable to pay taxes"]}
{"id":"ghi-016","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":2,"q":"What did the 1601 Poor Law do?","o":["Made each parish collect a poor rate to help its poor","Gave all poor people free food from the queen","Ended all help for the old and sick","Sent the poor to colonies in America"],"a":0,"x":"The 1601 Act made parishes responsible for their own poor, funded by a compulsory poor rate on householders. It required overseers to provide work for the able and relief for the impotent poor.","w":["","Help came from local poor rates paid by householders, not from the queen","Opposite: the Act required relief for the old and sick","Colonies were not the answer; the poor were helped within their parish"]}
{"id":"ghi-017","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":2,"q":"Why did Puritans criticise the theatres of Elizabethan England?","o":["They thought plays were too expensive","They thought plays were immoral and drew people away from church","They thought plays should only be about the queen","They thought actors were spies for Spain"],"a":1,"x":"Many Puritans said plays encouraged idleness and immorality. City authorities also worried about disorder and plague in crowded playhouses, and often tried to restrict them.","w":["Cost wasn't the Puritan complaint; they objected on moral grounds","","Made up; Puritans objected to the morals of plays, not who they were about","Mixes up fear of Spanish spies with the Puritan worry about immorality"]}
{"id":"ghi-018","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":2,"q":"Who was most likely to be accused of witchcraft in Elizabethan England?","o":["A rich merchant in a big town","A young nobleman at court","A priest from the local church","A poor, elderly woman living alone who had quarrelled with neighbours"],"a":3,"x":"Accusations often fell on poor women, many widows, who depended on charity. If a neighbour then suffered bad luck, such as a sick child, they could be blamed.","w":["Rich merchants had status and protection, so were rarely accused","Nobles at court were powerful and seldom accused","Priests were respected figures; accusations targeted the poor and vulnerable",""]}
{"id":"ghi-019","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":1,"q":"Which captain became the first Englishman to sail around the world, in 1577-80?","o":["Walter Raleigh","John Hawkins","Francis Drake","Martin Frobisher"],"a":2,"x":"Drake sailed the Golden Hind round the world and returned with Spanish treasure. Elizabeth knighted him in 1581.","w":["Raleigh is linked to Roanoke, not sailing round the world","Hawkins is known for slave-trading voyages, not circumnavigation","","Frobisher searched for the North-West Passage, not a round-the-world trip"]}
{"id":"ghi-020","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":2,"q":"What did John White find when he returned to the Roanoke colony in 1590?","o":["The settlers had gone and the word 'Croatoan' was carved on a post","The colony had grown into a rich town","The settlers had been killed by Spanish soldiers","The settlers had sailed home to England"],"a":0,"x":"White had left in 1587 and was delayed by the Armada. In 1590 he found the houses empty and 'Croatoan' carved on a post, and the settlers were never found.","w":["","Roanoke failed; White found empty houses, not a thriving town","No evidence of Spanish attack; the carved word pointed elsewhere","No sign they went home; the settlers were never found"]}
{"id":"ghi-021","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":1,"q":"Why were many medieval castles built on hills or next to rivers?","o":["It made them cheaper to heat","The king only allowed castles there","The sites were easy to defend and gave a good view of the land","They were nearer to the church"],"a":2,"x":"A hill or river made attack harder and gave a good lookout. A historian studying a site asks why it is where it is, looking at its geography and who it was meant to control.","w":["Heating wasn't the reason; defence and lookout were","Kings didn't limit castles to such sites; builders chose them for defence","","Being near a church had no bearing on castle siting"]}
{"id":"ghi-022","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":2,"q":"Which would best show how a historic site changed over time?","o":["A guidebook from last year","A single photograph taken today","The opinion of one visitor","Plans, maps and paintings from different dates"],"a":3,"x":"Comparing sources from different dates shows what was added, changed or lost. A modern photograph shows only the site as it is now.","w":["A modern guidebook shows the site only as it is now","One photo is a snapshot of today and shows no change over time","One opinion can't show how a site changed over time",""]}
{"id":"ghi-023","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"When a historian asks how typical a site is, they are comparing it with:","o":["Other similar sites from the same period","Sites from completely different countries","Modern buildings in the same town","The first building ever built"],"a":0,"x":"To judge whether a site is typical, historians compare features such as layout, size and use with similar sites of the time. Unusual features show where the site is special.","w":["","Sites from other countries aren't like-for-like, so can't show how typical it is","Modern buildings are from a different period, so can't judge typicality","One very early building from another time can't show what was normal"]}
{"id":"ghi-024","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"Which source would best tell historians who used a building and for what purpose?","o":["The colour of the stone","Documents such as household accounts and inventories","A modern map of the car park","The number of visitors per year"],"a":1,"x":"Written records of the time can show who lived and worked at a site and what they did. Physical remains alone often cannot show how rooms were used.","w":["Stone colour says nothing about who used the building or why","","A modern car park map tells you nothing about past use","Visitor numbers show popularity today, not the original purpose"]}
{"id":"ghi-025","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":2,"q":"A reconstruction drawing of a ruined building is useful but should be treated carefully because it is:","o":["Based on evidence and informed guesses, so it is an interpretation","A photograph taken at the time","Always based on written plans","Created by the people who lived there"],"a":0,"x":"Reconstructions use surviving remains and documents, but gaps are filled by judgement, so different experts may draw different versions.","w":["","A reconstruction is drawn later, so it can't be a photograph from the time.","Plans rarely survive in full, so artists also use remains and guesses.","It is drawn by later artists or experts, not by the people who lived there."]}
{"id":"ghi-026","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":3,"q":"What is one limitation of using only the physical remains of a site as evidence?","o":["They always show the site as it was in its first year","Parts have decayed or been rebuilt, and they show little about people's thoughts","They cannot be visited by historians","They are written down by the owners"],"a":1,"x":"Remains survive unevenly and later changes may hide earlier ones. They show what was built and used, but not the beliefs and feelings of the people involved, so historians combine them with written sources.","w":["Remains are often altered or rebuilt, so they rarely show the first year.","","Historians can visit sites; the limits are decay and what remains reveal.","Remains are buildings and objects, not writing, so no owner wrote them down."]}
{"id":"ghi-027","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":1,"q":"Which country sold the Louisiana Territory to the USA in 1803?","o":["Spain","Britain","Mexico","France"],"a":3,"x":"Napoleon sold Louisiana to the USA in 1803 under President Jefferson, roughly doubling the size of the country.","w":["Spain once owned it but had handed it back to France before 1803.","Britain never owned Louisiana; France sold it.","Mexico was not yet independent in 1803 and did not own Louisiana.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-028","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":2,"q":"How did the invention of the cotton gin (1793) affect the South?","o":["It ended the need for enslaved workers","It made the North grow more cotton","It banned cotton farming in the South","Cotton became more profitable, increasing the demand for enslaved workers"],"a":3,"x":"Eli Whitney's gin cleaned cotton much faster. More cotton could be sold, so planters spread across the South and relied on more enslaved workers to grow it.","w":["Back to front: the gin made cotton more profitable, so more enslaved workers were needed.","The gin boosted cotton growing in the South, not the North.","Cotton farming expanded hugely in the South after the gin; it wasn't banned.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-029","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":2,"q":"What did the Indian Removal Act of 1830 allow the US government to do?","o":["Move Native Americans from the east to land west of the Mississippi","Give Native Americans full citizenship","Ban white settlers from Native land","Pay Native Americans for crops"],"a":0,"x":"Under President Andrew Jackson, Native peoples were forced west. The Cherokee removal in 1838-39 became known as the Trail of Tears, and many died on the journey.","w":["","Citizenship wasn't given; the Act enabled forced removal.","The opposite: it opened Native land to white settlement.","Nothing about paying for crops; the Act was about forced removal west."]}
{"id":"ghi-030","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":1,"q":"For Plains Indians, what was the most important use of the buffalo?","o":["It gave food, shelter, clothing and tools","It was kept only for trade with settlers","It was used only for religious ceremonies","It was hunted only for sport"],"a":0,"x":"Plains peoples used almost every part of the buffalo: meat for food, hides for tipis and clothing, and bones for tools. Its loss in the 1870s-80s was devastating.","w":["","Plains peoples used buffalo themselves, not just for trade with settlers.","Ceremonies mattered, but buffalo were used far more widely in daily life.","Plains peoples hunted buffalo to survive, not for sport."]}
{"id":"ghi-031","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did the Mormons travel west to Utah in the 1840s?","o":["To search for gold in California","To join the US army","To escape persecution and build their own community","To trade with Native Americans"],"a":2,"x":"Mormons had been driven out of several states, and their leader Joseph Smith was killed in 1844. Brigham Young led them to the Great Salt Lake Valley in 1847.","w":["That was the 1849 Gold Rush; Mormons went to Utah seeking safety.","They weren't joining the army; they wanted freedom to practise their faith.","","Trade wasn't the aim; they fled persecution to build their own community."]}
{"id":"ghi-032","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":1,"q":"Which event in April 1861 began the American Civil War?","o":["The Battle of Gettysburg","Lee's surrender at Appomattox","The Confederate attack on Fort Sumter","The assassination of Lincoln"],"a":2,"x":"Southern forces fired on Fort Sumter in South Carolina after seven states had left the Union following Lincoln's election. Gettysburg was in 1863 and Appomattox in 1865.","w":["Gettysburg was in 1863, two years into the war.","Appomattox in 1865 ended the war rather than began it.","","Lincoln was assassinated in 1865, near the end of the war, not the start."]}
{"id":"ghi-033","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":3,"q":"Why did many freed African Americans become sharecroppers after the Civil War?","o":["They had no land or money, so they farmed another's land for a share of the crop","The government gave each family 40 acres","Northern law forced them to work on farms","They wanted to join the army"],"a":0,"x":"Most freed people were given no land. Sharecropping often kept them in debt to landowners, so economic freedom was limited even after slavery ended.","w":["","'40 acres and a mule' was briefly promised but mostly never delivered.","Northern law didn't force them; lack of land and money pushed them into it.","Joining the army wasn't the reason; lack of land and money was."]}
{"id":"ghi-034","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":1,"q":"Under the Homestead Act of 1862, what could settlers receive?","o":["A free railway ticket west","A loan to buy cattle","A job working for the army","160 acres of land if they farmed it for five years"],"a":3,"x":"The Homestead Act offered 160 acres to anyone who lived on and farmed the land for five years. Many settlers moved onto the Plains, and some struggled with drought and poor soil.","w":["Railway companies sold land cheaply, but the Act gave land, not tickets.","The Act gave land, not loans for cattle.","It offered farmland, not army jobs.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-035","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":2,"q":"Where were the Union Pacific and Central Pacific railroads joined in 1869?","o":["Chicago, Illinois","Omaha, Nebraska","Promontory Summit, Utah","Sacramento, California"],"a":2,"x":"The first transcontinental railroad was completed at Promontory Summit in Utah on 10 May 1869. Travel across the continent fell from months to about a week.","w":["Chicago was a rail hub, but far from where the two lines met.","Omaha was the Union Pacific's starting point, not where the lines joined.","","Sacramento was where the Central Pacific began, not where the lines met."]}
{"id":"ghi-036","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":3,"q":"Why did the discovery of gold in the Black Hills in 1874 lead to war with the Sioux?","o":["The railroad company demanded the land for its tracks","The railroad refused to carry the gold","Mormon settlers had already claimed it","Miners flooded in to land that the 1868 treaty had promised to the Sioux"],"a":3,"x":"The Black Hills were sacred to the Sioux and protected by the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868. Fighting in 1876 included the defeat of Custer's troops at Little Bighorn.","w":["The railroad wasn't the cause; miners broke the treaty by entering Sioux land.","The issue was land and the treaty, not carrying gold.","Mormons settled in Utah, not the Black Hills; miners were the intruders.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-037","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":3,"q":"What was the aim of the Dawes Act of 1887?","o":["To give reservations independence","To return land taken by settlers","To stop buffalo hunting","To break up reservation land and make Native Americans into farmers"],"a":3,"x":"The Dawes Act divided reservation land into individual plots. It aimed to assimilate Native Americans, but it reduced the land they held and weakened tribal life.","w":["Opposite: it broke up reservations and cut tribal land, not gave independence.","Reversed: Native Americans lost land under the Act, they did not regain it.","Mixes it up with buffalo slaughter; the Act was about land ownership, not hunting.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-038","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":2,"q":"What was the effect of the Supreme Court's Plessy v Ferguson decision in 1896?","o":["It ended segregation in schools","It gave Black Americans the vote","It allowed segregation if facilities were 'separate but equal'","It banned the Ku Klux Klan"],"a":2,"x":"The ruling said that separate facilities for Black and white people were legal if they were equal. In practice they were rarely equal, and it supported Jim Crow laws until the 1950s.","w":["Mixes it up with Brown v Board (1954); Plessy allowed segregation, not ended it.","Voting rights were a separate issue; Plessy was about segregated facilities.","","Nothing to do with the Klan; the ruling backed segregation and Jim Crow."]}
{"id":"ghi-039","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":1,"q":"Which event in February 1933 did Hitler blame on the Communists?","o":["The Munich Putsch","The Night of the Long Knives","The Enabling Act","The Reichstag fire"],"a":3,"x":"The Reichstag building burned on 27 February 1933. Hitler used it to persuade Hindenburg to suspend civil rights and arrest Communists.","w":["The Munich Putsch was Hitler's failed 1923 coup, not 1933.","The Night of the Long Knives was June 1934, a purge of the SA.","The Enabling Act came after the fire, in March 1933; it wasn't blamed on Communists.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-040","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":3,"q":"What did the Enabling Act of March 1933 allow Hitler to do?","o":["Become president as well as chancellor","Ban all churches in Germany","Hold a new Reichstag election every year","Make laws without the Reichstag's approval for four years"],"a":3,"x":"The Enabling Act passed on 23 March 1933 after Communists were barred and other parties were intimidated. It gave Hitler's government legal power to rule by decree.","w":["He became Führer after Hindenburg died in 1934; the Act was about lawmaking.","The Act gave power to make laws; churches were pressured, not banned outright.","Opposite: it removed the Reichstag's role rather than adding elections.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-041","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":1,"q":"What was the Gestapo?","o":["The Nazi youth movement","The Nazi secret state police","The German army","The Nazi propaganda ministry"],"a":1,"x":"The Gestapo, led by Himmler and Heydrich, spied on and arrested opponents of the regime. Many arrests came from tip-offs by ordinary citizens.","w":["That describes the Hitler Youth; the Gestapo was the secret police.","","The army was the Wehrmacht; the Gestapo was a police force.","Propaganda was Goebbels' ministry; the Gestapo spied and arrested."]}
{"id":"ghi-042","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did Pastor Martin Niemöller help form the Confessing Church in 1934?","o":["To support the Hitler Youth","To oppose Nazi control of the Protestant Church","To help Hitler win the 1934 vote","To escape from Germany"],"a":1,"x":"Niemöller opposed the pro-Nazi 'German Christians'. He was arrested in 1937 and spent years in concentration camps.","w":["Opposite: Niemöller opposed Nazi interference and didn't back the Hitler Youth.","","He was against Nazi control, so he wouldn't help Hitler win a vote.","He stayed in Germany and was arrested in 1937; his aim was to resist, not flee."]}
{"id":"ghi-043","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":1,"q":"What did the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 do?","o":["Forced Jews to leave Germany at once","Removed Jews' German citizenship and banned Jewish-'Aryan' marriage","Closed all Jewish shops","Created the Hitler Youth"],"a":1,"x":"The Nuremberg Laws defined who was Jewish and made them second-class citizens. They were a major step in the Nazis' persecution of Jews.","w":["Forced emigration came later; these laws removed rights but didn't expel Jews at once.","","Shop boycotts and Kristallnacht targeted businesses; the Nuremberg Laws did not.","The Hitler Youth was founded in the 1920s; it is unrelated to these laws."]}
{"id":"ghi-044","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":2,"q":"What did the Nazis use as an excuse for the Kristallnacht attacks in November 1938?","o":["A Jewish attack on the Reichstag","The murder of a German diplomat in Paris by a Jewish teenager","The failure of a Jewish business","A Jewish plot against Hitler's life"],"a":1,"x":"Herschel Grynszpan shot Ernst vom Rath. The Nazis organised attacks on synagogues and Jewish shops on 9-10 November, and thousands of Jewish men were arrested.","w":["Mixes it up with the 1933 Reichstag fire, which was blamed on Communists.","","Invented; the excuse was a shooting in Paris, not a business failure.","The 1944 bomb plot was by army officers, not Jews; wrong event."]}
{"id":"ghi-045","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":3,"q":"Why did Goebbels call for 'total war' in February 1943?","o":["After Stalingrad, he wanted all people and resources put into the war","Germany had just won at Stalingrad","Britain had invaded Germany","The Nazis wanted to end the war"],"a":0,"x":"Defeat at Stalingrad shocked Germany. In his Sportpalast speech Goebbels called on the nation to mobilise everyone and everything for war, including women and the closure of non-essential businesses.","w":["","Germany lost at Stalingrad; that defeat is why total war was called.","Britain never invaded Germany in 1943; the trigger was defeat in the USSR.","Total war meant fighting harder, not ending the war."]}
{"id":"ghi-046","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":2,"q":"What happened to the 20 July 1944 bomb plot against Hitler?","o":["The bomb killed Hitler and the war ended","The bomb failed to go off and the plot was never discovered","The bomb exploded but Hitler survived and the plotters were punished","The army took over the government"],"a":2,"x":"Claus von Stauffenberg placed a bomb at Hitler's headquarters, but Hitler survived. Stauffenberg was shot and many others were executed.","w":["Hitler survived and the war carried on until 1945.","The bomb did explode, and the plotters were quickly found and executed.","","The army did not take power; the coup failed and the Nazis stayed in control."]}
{"id":"ghi-047","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":2,"q":"What was the main purpose of the Wannsee Conference in January 1942?","o":["To coordinate the Nazi plan to murder Europe's Jews","To plan the invasion of Britain","To end the war with the Soviet Union","To decide where Jews could settle in Germany"],"a":0,"x":"Senior Nazis, led by Reinhard Heydrich, met in Berlin to organise the 'Final Solution' across Europe. Mass murder by the Einsatzgruppen had already begun.","w":["","The invasion of Britain was a 1940 plan; Wannsee was about the Final Solution.","Wannsee was about Jews, not peace; the war with the USSR went on.","Settling Jews in Germany wasn't the plan; it was murder across Europe."]}
{"id":"ghi-048","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":1,"q":"Which marshal led the collaborationist government in southern France after 1940?","o":["Charles de Gaulle","Ferdinand Foch","Joseph Joffre","Philippe Pétain"],"a":3,"x":"After France's defeat in 1940, the Vichy government under Pétain cooperated with Germany. De Gaulle led the Free French in exile.","w":["De Gaulle led the Free French in exile, opposing Vichy.","Foch was a First World War Allied commander who died in 1929.","Joffre was a First World War French commander who died in 1931.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-049","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":1,"q":"Many monasteries were cleaner than medieval towns. Which feature of monastery life helped most to keep them clean?","o":["A rule that monks must never touch anyone who was ill","Running water that carried waste away from toilets and washing areas","Regular checks by royal health inspectors","Medicines based on the idea of germs"],"a":1,"x":"Many monasteries had piped water and drains. These flushed waste away from the toilets and washing areas. Towns rarely had this. Monks also ran infirmaries and cared for the sick, so they did not avoid them.","w":["Monks ran infirmaries and cared for the sick, so this is wrong. Do not confuse monasteries with later quarantine rules.","","There were no royal health inspectors in the Middle Ages. Monasteries ran their own rules and systems.","Germ theory was not developed until the 1860s, so medieval medicines were not based on it."]}
{"id":"ghi-050","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":1,"q":"Some medieval doctors used bloodletting on plague patients. Which belief did this treatment come from?","o":["It removed germs from the blood","It was a punishment that won God's forgiveness","It balanced the four humours by removing extra blood","It made the patient sweat out bad air they had breathed in"],"a":2,"x":"Doctors followed the theory of the four humours from Hippocrates and Galen. They thought illness came from an imbalance. Cutting a vein was meant to remove extra blood and restore balance. It did not remove germs.","w":["Germs were not known about until the 1860s, so doctors could not aim to remove them.","This mixes it up with flagellants, who whipped themselves as penance. Bloodletting was a medical treatment.","","Bad air is miasma, a different idea. Bloodletting was linked to the humours, not to sweating out air."]}
{"id":"ghi-051","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":2,"q":"In the 1200s, London began piping spring water to a public fountain called the Great Conduit. Why was this a useful public health step?","o":["The pipes killed the germs in the water","It stopped the plague from reaching London","It meant people no longer needed to wash at all","It gave people a cleaner supply than the polluted Thames and Fleet rivers"],"a":3,"x":"Medieval rivers were used to dump rubbish, waste and animal guts. Spring water from a conduit was cleaner to drink and use. People did not know about germs, so the benefit was real but not understood.","w":["People did not know about germs, so no pipe was designed to kill them.","The plague arrived in 1348 and a water supply could not stop it. This confuses cleaner water with plague prevention.","Washing was still needed. A public supply made clean water easier to reach.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-052","s":"history","t":"ph-1","lv":3,"q":"In 1349 London was ordered to clear filth from its streets, yet plague still killed thousands. Which is the best explanation for why this did not stop it?","o":["It targeted foul air, but fleas mainly spread plague and people did not know this","The plague was carried in drinking water, which cleaning the streets ignored","Nobody in authority had any power to order streets to be cleaned","People thought the plague was only God's punishment, so nobody tried to clean anything"],"a":0,"x":"Cleaning followed the miasma idea that bad smells caused disease. The Black Death was spread mainly by fleas, usually carried by rats. People did not know this. The trap is thinking they did nothing: they acted, but on the wrong cause.","w":["","The plague was not carried mainly by water. Fleas spread it, and this wrongly blames the water supply.","Kings and town councils did issue orders about cleaning streets. Their power was limited, but it existed.","Many people believed in God's punishment and in miasma at once. Streets were cleaned, so this is wrong."]}
{"id":"ghi-053","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":1,"q":"In the 1665 plague in London, what was the job of the 'searchers'?","o":["Guarding the doors of houses that had been shut up","Killing stray dogs and cats in the streets","Examining dead bodies to decide if the cause was plague","Carrying the dead to plague pits at night"],"a":2,"x":"Searchers were often older women paid by the parish. They looked at bodies and reported the cause of death, which was counted in the Bills of Mortality. Do not mix them up with watchmen, dog killers or bearers.","w":["That was the watchmen's job; searchers inspected bodies, not doors.","Dog killers did this, hoping it would stop plague spreading; it was not the searchers' task.","","Bearers carried the bodies; searchers only reported the cause of death."]}
{"id":"ghi-054","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":2,"q":"What helped make gin cheap and easy to buy in England by the early 1700s?","o":["The government made French brandy cheaper to import","Only wealthy people were allowed to buy spirits","A new law in 1736 removed all tax from gin","A 1690 law let anyone distil grain spirits without a licence"],"a":3,"x":"In 1690 the government let anyone make spirits from English grain without a licence, and it restricted French brandy imports. This backed farmers, but led to cheap gin everywhere. The 1736 Act raised tax, not removed it.","w":["The government restricted French brandy imports, which pushed people towards home-made gin.","Gin was cheap and sold to rich and poor alike; there was no such limit.","The 1736 Act did the opposite: it added a high tax and licence fee to cut drinking.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-055","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about the Great Fire of London (1666) and the Great Plague (1665) is correct?","o":["The fire ended the plague by burning all the plague-carrying rats","The Rebuilding Act required brick or stone, but plague had already declined","The plague first broke out in the ruins of the city after the fire","The Rebuilding Act set up a Board of Health to inspect every street"],"a":1,"x":"The 1667 Rebuilding Act required brick or stone and wider streets, which cut fire risk. But plague deaths had already dropped by September 1666, and many poor areas did not burn. Do not say the fire ended the plague.","w":["Plague had already faded before the fire, and many poor suburbs were not burned, so the fire was not the cause.","","The plague came first, in 1665, and the fire followed in 1666.","The Act dealt with building materials and street width; no national Board of Health existed then."]}
{"id":"ghi-056","s":"history","t":"ph-2","lv":3,"q":"Why were plague orders such as shutting up houses often ineffective in 1665?","o":["Healthy people were trapped with the sick and fleas, and some hid cases or fled","Houses were shut up for only one day, so the sick were soon free again","The orders were ignored because only the king himself could enforce them","No watchmen were ever placed at the doors of infected houses"],"a":0,"x":"Shutting healthy people in with the sick, rats and fleas could increase deaths. Some hid cases or slipped out. The measure lasted forty days and was enforced locally by watchmen. Weak understanding of the cause limited every order.","w":["","Houses were shut up for forty days, not one; length was not the problem.","Local officials such as the Lord Mayor and parish officers enforced orders, not the king alone.","Watchmen were placed on infected houses; the order was enforced but flawed."]}
{"id":"ghi-057","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":1,"q":"What was the main message of Edwin Chadwick's 1842 report on the living conditions of the labouring population?","o":["Poor people were lazy, so their illness was their own fault","Dirty living conditions caused disease, and cleaning towns would save money","Tiny germs caused disease, so doctors needed new training","Smoke from factories caused most disease, so they should be shut down"],"a":1,"x":"Chadwick said filth and bad drains caused disease. He argued that clean water and sewers would cost less than looking after sick poor people. He believed in miasma, not germs, which is a common trap.","w":["Blames the poor. Chadwick argued that dirty surroundings, not laziness, caused disease.","","Germ theory came later. Chadwick blamed filth and bad smells, not germs, so this is anachronistic.","The report was about filth, drains and water. It did not call for factories to be shut."]}
{"id":"ghi-058","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":2,"q":"In 1845 a rich town councillor says: \"The government should not force people to pay for drains. Leave it to individuals.\" Which idea is he showing?","o":["Miasma theory","Germ theory","Chartism","Laissez-faire"],"a":3,"x":"Laissez-faire means the government should interfere as little as possible. Many people in power believed this, so they opposed taxes and compulsory laws for public health. This slowed reform until later in the century.","w":["Miasma was a belief about how disease spread, not about the role of government.","Germ theory explained disease. It said nothing about whether governments should act.","Chartism campaigned for votes for working men, not against paying for drains.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-059","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":2,"q":"The 1875 Public Health Act was more effective than the 1848 Act. What did the 1875 Act do?","o":["Made councils provide clean water, sewers and refuse collection","Let councils choose whether to act, as in 1848","Set up a new General Board of Health to run every town","Made every child be vaccinated against smallpox"],"a":0,"x":"The 1875 Act made local councils carry out key duties such as clean water, drainage and rubbish removal. It was compulsory, unlike the 1848 Act. The vaccination laws were separate Acts.","w":["","This describes the weakness of the 1848 Act. The 1875 Act was compulsory.","The General Board of Health belonged to 1848 and was closed in 1858.","Compulsory vaccination came from separate Vaccination Acts, not the 1875 Public Health Act."]}
{"id":"ghi-060","s":"history","t":"ph-3","lv":3,"q":"In 1858 the \"Great Stink\" from the Thames led Parliament to approve a new London sewer system quickly. Why did it act so fast?","o":["Parliament had accepted Snow's proof that water spread cholera","A new cholera outbreak that year had killed several MPs","Many MPs believed bad smells caused disease, and the smell reached them","The 1848 Act forced Parliament to build sewers when the river was polluted"],"a":2,"x":"The smell was so bad at the Houses of Parliament that MPs struggled to work. Many still believed in miasma, so they feared disease. Bazalgette's sewers were then built. Snow's water theory was not yet widely accepted.","w":["Snow's idea was not widely accepted in 1858. MPs acted out of fear of smells.","No outbreak killed MPs in 1858. The fear was of disease from the smell.","","The 1848 Act did not cover London and was mostly not compulsory, so it forced nothing."]}
{"id":"ghi-061","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":1,"q":"Why was it so hard for doctors to fight the Spanish Influenza epidemic of 1918-19 in Britain?","o":["Antibiotics existed but were too expensive for most people","The cause was unknown, so there was no vaccine or proven cure","The NHS was too busy with wounded soldiers to treat civilians","The flu only affected soldiers, so the government ignored it"],"a":1,"x":"Flu viruses were not identified until the 1930s. In 1918 doctors had no vaccine and no proven cure. The trap is thinking antibiotics could have helped: they were not available, and they do not work on viruses.","w":["Antibiotics were not in use in 1918, and they do not work on viruses anyway.","","The NHS did not exist until 1948, so it could not have been busy in 1918.","Spanish Influenza killed many civilians, often young adults, so it was not just a soldiers' illness."]}
{"id":"ghi-062","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":2,"q":"Why did the government ban smoking in enclosed public places in England in 2007?","o":["Evidence showed second-hand smoke harmed the health of non-smokers","Smoking from pubs was found to be causing smog in cities","Tobacco companies had stopped making cigarettes","It was a way to raise money for the NHS through higher cigarette tax"],"a":0,"x":"By 2007 research showed passive smoking caused illness in non-smokers such as pub staff. The ban protected them. The trap is mixing it up with the Clean Air Act 1956, which dealt with coal smoke and smog.","w":["","Smog was the issue behind the Clean Air Act of 1956, not the 2007 smoking ban.","Tobacco companies carried on selling cigarettes; the ban was about where people could smoke.","The ban was a health measure. It did not raise tax money for the NHS."]}
{"id":"ghi-063","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":2,"q":"What did the Housing Act of 1919 (the Addison Act) do to improve living conditions after the First World War?","o":["It made private landlords give their homes to tenants for free","It banned back-to-back houses for the first time","It set up the NHS to treat people living in poor housing","It gave councils government money to build new houses for working families"],"a":3,"x":"The Addison Act of 1919 gave councils a government subsidy to build houses, as part of 'homes fit for heroes'. The trap is the NHS: it came in 1948, and back-to-backs were banned in 1909.","w":["Landlords kept their property; the Act was about councils building new homes.","Back-to-back houses were banned by an earlier Act, in 1909.","The NHS was created in 1948, not in 1919, and it was about healthcare rather than housing.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-064","s":"history","t":"ph-4","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best explains a difference between the response to Spanish Influenza (1918-19) and the response to AIDS in the 1980s?","o":["In 1918 the government used TV adverts, but in the 1980s it used none","A vaccine for AIDS was found by 1990, but there was none for flu in 1918","Scientists identified HIV within a few years, but the flu virus was unknown in 1918","The government ignored AIDS, but it ran national campaigns about Spanish Influenza"],"a":2,"x":"HIV was identified by scientists within a few years of AIDS appearing, so a national information campaign could follow. In 1918 the flu virus was unknown. The trap is swapping the two: TV adverts belong to the 1980s.","w":["Television did not exist in 1918; it was the 1980s AIDS campaign that used TV adverts.","No AIDS vaccine has ever been found; the 1980s response relied on prevention advice.","","The government ran a major AIDS campaign in 1986-87, while its 1918 action was limited."]}
{"id":"ghi-065","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":1,"q":"Why did the fast growth of industrial towns in the early 1800s make public health worse?","o":["Doctors refused to work in towns","Workers lived in cramped housing with no clean water or sewers","The government built too many hospitals","Most people moved back to the countryside"],"a":1,"x":"Towns grew faster than councils could plan them. Cheap, crowded housing with shared privies and dirty water spread diseases like cholera and typhoid. The trap is blaming doctors or hospitals instead of overcrowding and poor sanitation.","w":["Doctors were not refusing to work in towns; the problem was dirty, crowded living conditions.","","Hospitals were not the problem. Little government action was taken on housing and sanitation at first.","People kept moving into towns for factory work, not back to the countryside."]}
{"id":"ghi-066","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":2,"q":"Why is the 1875 Public Health Act seen as more important than the 1848 Public Health Act?","o":["It set up the NHS to treat everyone for free","It made vaccination of babies compulsory","It created the General Board of Health","It forced councils to provide clean water, sewers and drains"],"a":3,"x":"The 1848 Act was mostly voluntary, so many councils did nothing. The 1875 Act made local councils carry out sanitary duties such as clean water and sewers. The trap is mixing it up with the 1848 Board of Health.","w":["The NHS only began in 1948, many decades later.","Compulsory vaccination of babies came earlier, in the 1853 Vaccination Act.","The General Board of Health was created by the 1848 Act, not the 1875 Act.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-067","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":3,"q":"Reformers in the 1840s wrongly believed in miasma (disease from bad air). How did this belief still help improve public health?","o":["It led them to clean up waste and build sewers to remove smells","It led them to make vaccination compulsory","It led them to find the microbe that causes cholera","It led them to test water supplies for germs"],"a":0,"x":"Miasma believers like Chadwick wanted to remove rubbish and sewage to get rid of foul smells. This also removed real sources of disease, so wrong ideas could still lead to useful change. The trap is thinking only correct theories help.","w":["","Vaccination was linked to smallpox and Jenner's work, not to the miasma idea.","Koch identified the cholera microbe in 1883, long after miasma-based reforms began.","Miasma believers blamed smells, not germs; testing water for germs came after germ theory."]}
{"id":"ghi-068","s":"history","t":"ph-5","lv":2,"q":"Even after the National Insurance Act of 1911, many poor families went without doctors. What was the main reason?","o":["It only covered people over 70","It was only for middle-class workers","It covered the insured worker but not their wife and children","It charged a fee at every doctor's visit"],"a":2,"x":"The 1911 Act gave some workers free GP care paid for by insurance. It did not cover their families, who still had to pay. The trap is confusing it with old-age pensions or thinking it was a full NHS.","w":["Old-age pensions began in 1908 for the over-70s. This is a different reform.","The Act covered low-paid manual workers, not just the middle class.","","Insured workers received free GP treatment. The gap was that their families were not covered."]}
{"id":"ghi-069","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":1,"q":"Who had the power to call and dissolve Parliament during Elizabeth I's reign?","o":["The Speaker of the House of Commons","The Privy Council, by a majority vote","The queen alone","The Lord Chancellor and the bishops"],"a":2,"x":"Parliament met only when Elizabeth summoned it, usually to raise taxes or pass laws. She could dissolve it and veto bills. The trap is thinking Parliament could meet and rule itself.","w":["The Speaker led debates in the Commons but could not call or end Parliament; that was the monarch's right.","The Council advised the queen but did not vote on when Parliament met; the decision was hers.","","The Lord Chancellor and bishops had roles in Parliament but no power to summon it; only the queen could."]}
{"id":"ghi-070","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":1,"q":"Why were Justices of the Peace (JPs) so useful to Elizabeth's government?","o":["They were local gentry who worked unpaid, so the crown saved money","They were paid royal officials sent from London to each county","They were elected by local people to speak against royal policy","They were Privy Councillors who lived at court"],"a":0,"x":"JPs were local landowners who kept order, enforced laws and ran the Poor Law without pay. They gained status and the queen saved money. The trap is thinking local government was run by paid officials from London.","w":["","Elizabeth had no paid county officials from London; JPs were local men, so this confuses them with a modern civil service.","JPs were chosen by the crown, not elected; they carried out royal orders rather than opposing them.","Privy Councillors advised the queen at the centre; JPs were local gentry in the counties."]}
{"id":"ghi-071","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":2,"q":"Elizabeth went on summer progresses to the homes of nobles and gentry. Which statement best explains why this helped her control the country?","o":["She collected taxes in person from every county she visited","She held Parliament in the countryside to win over local MPs","She trained local soldiers in each county for the militia","She was seen by subjects and kept nobles loyal, at her hosts' cost"],"a":3,"x":"Progresses let ordinary people see the queen and let her keep an eye on powerful families. Hosts paid for her large court, so it was cheap for the crown. It was also propaganda. Progresses were not tax-collecting trips.","w":["Taxes were collected through Parliament and local officials, not by the queen on tour; this mixes up purpose and method.","Parliament met in London or Westminster; progresses were visits to private houses, not parliamentary sessions.","Militias were organised by local lords and officials; progresses were about image and loyalty, not drilling troops.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-072","s":"history","t":"el-1","lv":3,"q":"In 1601 MPs complained loudly about monopolies. How did Elizabeth respond?","o":["She dissolved Parliament and imprisoned the MPs who spoke out","She promised to end the worst abuses and thanked MPs in her Golden Speech","She agreed to a law that took away her power to grant monopolies","She ignored the complaints and refused to speak to Parliament"],"a":1,"x":"Faced with angry MPs, Elizabeth gave way on the worst abuses and promised reform. In her Golden Speech of November 1601 she thanked MPs. She kept the right to grant monopolies, so she compromised without losing power.","w":["She imprisoned Peter Wentworth in 1593, but in 1601 she compromised instead; this mixes up two parliaments.","","No law removed her power to grant monopolies; she kept that right and only promised to fix abuses.","She spoke in person to about 140 MPs in the Golden Speech, so this wrongly shows her as stubborn."]}
{"id":"ghi-073","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":1,"q":"In 1581 Parliament punished Catholics who refused to attend Church of England services with a much bigger fine. How much was it?","o":["12 pence a week","£2 a month","£20 a month","£200 a year"],"a":2,"x":"The 1581 Act raised the recusancy fine to £20 a month, a huge sum. The trap is 12 pence a week, which was the old fine from the 1559 Act of Uniformity.","w":["This was the original 1559 fine. The 1581 Act raised it a great deal.","Far too low. The 1581 fine was meant to ruin wealthy Catholics, so it was much larger.","","Wrong amount and period. The 1581 fine was £20 a month, which comes to about £240 a year."]}
{"id":"ghi-074","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did some English Catholics see Mary, Queen of Scots as a rival to Elizabeth I?","o":["She was Elizabeth's half-sister and had ruled England before her","They thought Elizabeth was illegitimate, so Mary was the rightful queen","She had been crowned queen of England in 1568","She was married to Philip II of Spain"],"a":1,"x":"Many Catholics did not accept Henry VIII's marriage to Anne Boleyn, so they saw Elizabeth as illegitimate. Mary was a Catholic with a strong claim. The trap is mixing her up with Mary I.","w":["This is Mary I, not Mary, Queen of Scots. Confusing the two Marys is the slip.","","She was Queen of Scotland and fled to England in 1568. She was never crowned in England.","Mary I married Philip II, not Mary, Queen of Scots. Again the two Marys are mixed up."]}
{"id":"ghi-075","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":2,"q":"In 1587 Drake attacked the Spanish port of Cadiz. What was the main effect of this raid?","o":["It destroyed the Armada before it left Spain","It captured Philip II's treasure fleet","It made Philip agree to peace with England","It destroyed many ships and supplies, so the Armada was delayed"],"a":3,"x":"Drake burned many Spanish ships and supplies, so the Armada sailed a year late. The trap is thinking it ended the threat. The Armada still sailed in 1588.","w":["The main Armada was not at Cadiz and still sailed in 1588. This overstates the raid.","Drake did capture a treasure ship that year, but not the treasure fleet. This is not the raid's main effect.","Philip did not seek peace. He carried on preparing the invasion, so this is the wrong result.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-076","s":"history","t":"el-2","lv":3,"q":"In 1588 the Armada was meant to carry the Duke of Parma's army across the Channel. Why did this plan fail?","o":["It could not safely meet Parma, as Dutch and English ships blocked the coast","Parma's soldiers had refused to fight for Philip II","The Armada carried no soldiers, only cannon","Parma's army was defeated by Elizabeth's troops at Tilbury"],"a":0,"x":"The Armada had to join Parma's army on the Dutch coast. There was no deep-water port, and the Dutch and English blocked it. The Armada was then driven off at Gravelines. Tilbury was only a defensive camp.","w":["","Parma's army did not rebel. It was ready but could not be picked up safely, so this is invented.","The Armada carried thousands of soldiers. It was also meant to collect more, so this is wrong.","Tilbury was where Elizabeth spoke to her troops, and no battle took place there. Parma never reached England."]}
{"id":"ghi-077","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":1,"q":"Why did enclosure add to unemployment in Elizabethan England?","o":["Landowners built factories on farmland, and these closed within a year","Farmers were banned from selling wool abroad, so they sacked workers","Landowners fenced off land for sheep, which needed fewer workers than crops","The queen taxed farm workers so heavily that they gave up work"],"a":2,"x":"Enclosure meant fencing off open or common land. Many landowners used it for sheep, which needed far fewer workers than arable farming. Labourers lost work and often drifted into poverty. The trap is thinking enclosure meant factories.","w":["There were no factories on farmland then; this muddles Elizabethan England with the later Industrial Revolution.","Wool was a major export and was not banned; enclosure was about changing land use.","","No such tax on farm workers existed; the job losses came from changes in land use."]}
{"id":"ghi-078","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":2,"q":"Between 1550 and 1600, food prices rose much faster than wages. What was the effect on a farm labourer whose pay stayed much the same?","o":["His wages bought less food, so he could slip into poverty","He was better off, because his wage had not changed","He bought the same food, as higher prices hit only the rich","The parish automatically raised his pay to match prices"],"a":0,"x":"This is inflation. When prices rise faster than wages, the same pay buys less. Many labourers could no longer feed their families. The trap is thinking an unchanged wage means an unchanged standard of living.","w":["","Confuses a steady wage with steady buying power; with rising prices, the same wage buys less.","Rising food prices hit the poor hardest, as they spent most of their money on food.","Parishes did not raise wages; they gave relief to the poor, and only in need."]}
{"id":"ghi-079","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":2,"q":"Which statement best describes a married woman in a middling Elizabethan household, such as a merchant's or a yeoman's?","o":["She kept her own property and could sit as a magistrate","She was expected to leave home to work for wages in a factory","She was seen as equal to her husband under the law","She ran the home and its food, but under her husband's authority"],"a":3,"x":"A wife managed the home, servants and food, and often made ale and remedies. Law and custom made the husband head of the household, and a wife's property usually passed to him on marriage. The trap is assuming equality.","w":["Married women's property usually passed to their husbands, and women could not be magistrates.","Factories did not exist in Elizabethan England; the home was the main place of women's work.","The husband was the legal head of the family; wives were not equal under the law.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-080","s":"history","t":"el-3","lv":3,"q":"In 1602 a parish overseer deals with a widow too old to work, an orphan child and a fit man who refuses work. Which matches the 1601 Poor Law?","o":["Widow: house of correction; orphan: poor rate; fit man: relief at home","Widow: relief; orphan: apprenticeship; fit man: house of correction","Widow: apprenticeship; orphan: relief; fit man: hanged","Widow: relief; orphan: house of correction; fit man: given money to stay idle"],"a":1,"x":"The 1601 Act gave relief to the impotent poor, such as the old, and had pauper children apprenticed. Able-bodied people who refused work could be sent to a house of correction. The trap is mixing up the groups.","w":["Reverses the treatment: the old widow was 'deserving' and the idle fit man was punished.","","Apprenticeship was for children, not old widows, and refusing work was not punished by hanging under this Act.","Houses of correction were for idle adults, not orphans, and fit men were not paid to be idle."]}
{"id":"ghi-081","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":1,"q":"Many Elizabethan playhouses, such as the Rose and the Globe, were built on Bankside, south of the Thames. Why were they built there?","o":["The queen ordered that all theatres be built there","The City of London's leaders disliked plays and had little power outside the city","Actors needed to be near the sea to travel abroad","The Church of England said plays must be south of the river"],"a":1,"x":"Playhouses were put outside the City's control, in places like Southwark. City leaders feared disorder, plague and idleness, and could close theatres inside the walls. The trap is thinking the queen or Church chose the site.","w":["No order like this existed; Elizabeth enjoyed plays but did not choose where they were built.","","Invented reason; the sea played no part in choosing playhouse sites.","The Church did not set locations; the real issue was the City's hostility to plays."]}
{"id":"ghi-082","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":1,"q":"The 1563 Witchcraft Act made killing someone by witchcraft a crime punishable by death. How were convicted witches usually executed in Elizabethan England?","o":["Burned at the stake","Drowned in the village pond","Beheaded with an axe","Hanged"],"a":3,"x":"In England, convicted witches were normally hanged. Burning was used for heresy, and was common for witches in Scotland and parts of Europe. Mixing these up is a common trap.","w":["Mixes up England with Europe, or witchcraft with heresy; burning was not the usual English penalty.","Confuses an execution with 'swimming' a suspect as a test; drowning was not the legal punishment.","Beheading was mainly for nobles convicted of treason, not poor people accused of witchcraft.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-083","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":2,"q":"A Puritan writer in the 1580s complained that crowds spent Sundays at bear-baiting and plays instead of at church. Which Puritan belief best explains this complaint?","o":["Sunday should be kept holy for worship and Bible study, not pastimes","Bear-baiting and plays were banned by law on every day of the week","Bear-baiting and plays were old Catholic customs that must end","Ordinary people should never be allowed to enjoy any free time"],"a":0,"x":"Puritans wanted Sunday kept for worship and prayer. They said pastimes led people into idleness and sin. The trap is thinking such pastimes were illegal or that Puritans opposed all rest.","w":["","Wrong: bear-baiting and plays were legal and popular; the complaint was about Sunday, not a ban.","Misses the point: the complaint is about missing church on Sunday, not Catholic origins.","Exaggerates: Puritans objected to Sunday pastimes and idleness, not to all free time."]}
{"id":"ghi-084","s":"history","t":"el-4","lv":3,"q":"A poor widow begs food from a neighbour and is turned away. Soon after, the neighbour's cow dies. Which factor best explains why she might then be accused of witchcraft?","o":["The law ordered villagers to name one witch each year","She was a wealthy landowner whom neighbours envied","Neighbours felt guilty about refusing help, as poverty grew and charity declined","Witches were only found in towns, where Puritans were strongest"],"a":2,"x":"Poverty was rising and the old Church-based charity had weakened. People who refused help could feel guilty, then blame the beggar for later bad luck. The trap is thinking accusations came from the law, not village tensions.","w":["No such law existed; accusations usually came from neighbours, not from orders to find witches.","Accused witches were typically poor and dependent on others, not wealthy landowners.","","Witchcraft accusations were mostly in villages, and Puritan strength was not what caused them."]}
{"id":"ghi-086","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":2,"q":"Elizabethan merchants wanted silk and spices from the eastern Mediterranean, from lands ruled by the Ottoman Empire. Which company was set up to trade there?","o":["The Muscovy Company","The Merchant Adventurers","The East India Company","The Levant Company"],"a":3,"x":"The Levant Company traded with the Ottoman Empire in the eastern Mediterranean. The East India Company sailed round Africa to the East Indies, so it is the easy mix-up.","w":["This mixes up eastern trade routes, because the Muscovy Company traded with Russia, not the Ottoman Empire.","The Merchant Adventurers mostly sold English cloth in northern Europe, such as the Netherlands and Germany, not in the Mediterranean.","This assumes 'East' means Ottoman lands, but this company sailed round Africa to the East Indies from 1600.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-087","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":2,"q":"The first Roanoke colonists, led by Ralph Lane, went home with Drake in 1586. Why did they leave?","o":["Food was short and relations with the local people had broken down","Elizabeth ordered them home to prepare for the Spanish Armada","Spanish ships had attacked and destroyed the settlement","They had found gold and wanted to return home rich"],"a":0,"x":"The 1585 colony struggled to grow or find enough food and fell out with the local people. When Drake arrived in 1586 they left. The Armada did not come until 1588, a common date slip.","w":["","This is a date slip, because the Armada came in 1588, two years later, and no such order was given.","This assumes Spain was behind every English failure, but no Spanish attack ended this colony.","This assumes the dream of treasure came true, but the colonists found no gold at Roanoke."]}
{"id":"ghi-088","s":"history","t":"el-5","lv":3,"q":"The 1587 Roanoke colony under John White differed from the 1585 one. Which statement about the 1587 colony is correct?","o":["It was led by Drake and paid for by the government","It was a military base for attacking Spanish treasure ships","It included women and children and was meant as a permanent settlement","It was built on an island in the Caribbean Sea"],"a":2,"x":"The 1587 group included families, such as White's daughter Eleanor; his granddaughter Virginia Dare was born there. It aimed to settle for good, unlike the mostly male 1585 group. The trap is assuming it was another military outpost.","w":["This confuses the leaders, because John White led the 1587 colony and it was organised by Raleigh with private investors.","This describes ideas linked to the 1585 group, but the 1587 settlers were families planning to farm and stay.","","This misplaces the colony, because Roanoke was off the coast of what is now North Carolina, not in the Caribbean."]}
{"id":"ghi-089","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":1,"q":"Who founded Kenilworth Castle in the 1120s?","o":["Simon de Montfort, the rebel earl","Geoffrey de Clinton, an official serving Henry I","John of Gaunt, the Duke of Lancaster","Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester"],"a":1,"x":"Geoffrey de Clinton, a powerful official of Henry I, began the castle in the 1120s. The trap is picking a famous later owner. De Montfort, Gaunt and Dudley all came much later and changed the castle.","w":["De Montfort held the castle in the 1260s and was linked to its famous siege, but he did not found it.","","Gaunt rebuilt the castle in the later 1300s, over 200 years after it was founded.","Dudley was given the castle by Elizabeth I in the 1500s, so he changed it but did not found it."]}
{"id":"ghi-090","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":2,"q":"Kenilworth had a large lake called the Mere. How did it help the castle's defence?","o":["It was a sea inlet that let ships bring supplies","It was a dry ditch that stopped soldiers climbing the walls","It was a pond inside the keep that gave the garrison drinking water","It was a man-made lake that kept attackers away from much of the castle"],"a":3,"x":"The Mere was made by damming streams. The water covered a wide area around the castle, so attackers could not easily reach the walls. The trap is thinking it was a natural feature or a small moat.","w":["Kenilworth is far inland, so it had no sea inlet. The Mere was made by damming streams.","The Mere was a wide lake of water, not a dry ditch. Mixing it up with a ditch is a common slip.","The Mere lay outside the castle and covered a large area. It was not a small pond in the keep.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-091","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":2,"q":"In the later 1300s John of Gaunt built a new Great Hall at Kenilworth. What does this show about how the castle's use changed?","o":["It was becoming a grand home that showed off his wealth and power","It was being made stronger for a long siege like the one in 1266","It was being prepared for a visit by Elizabeth I","It was being turned into a prison for rebels"],"a":0,"x":"Gaunt was very rich and powerful. His large hall and fine apartments show the castle was becoming a comfortable palace as well as a fortress. The trap is assuming every change was for defence.","w":["","A grand hall with big windows did not help in a siege. The 1266 siege was over 100 years earlier.","Elizabeth I's visit was in 1575, about 200 years after Gaunt's building work.","Nothing about the Great Hall points to a prison. It was a place for feasts and show."]}
{"id":"ghi-092","s":"history","t":"hau-1","lv":3,"q":"Why were parts of Kenilworth Castle deliberately broken down around 1650, and what happened to the Mere?","o":["Elizabeth I ordered it, to weaken Dudley's family; the Mere was kept for hunting","Damage from the 1266 siege was left unrepaired; the Mere dried up by itself","Parliament wanted to stop it being used as a stronghold again; the Mere was drained","Victorian owners took down walls to sell the stone; the Mere became a canal"],"a":2,"x":"After the Civil War, Parliament had the castle slighted so it could not be used as a military base again. Walls were broken and the Mere was drained. The trap is mixing up the dates and the causes.","w":["Elizabeth I died in 1603, long before 1650. She did not order the damage, and the Mere was drained.","The 1266 siege was nearly 400 years earlier. The damage around 1650 was deliberate, not neglect, and the Mere was drained on purpose.","","The slighting was around 1650, about 200 years before Victorian times, and the Mere was drained, not made a canal."]}
{"id":"ghi-093","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":1,"q":"In 1266 Kenilworth held out for about six months against Henry III's army. Who was inside the castle?","o":["Supporters of King John","Supporters of Simon de Montfort","Parliamentarian soldiers","Men loyal to Robert Dudley"],"a":1,"x":"After the Battle of Evesham (1265), de Montfort's surviving supporters held Kenilworth against Henry III. The Dictum of Kenilworth was issued during the siege. The trap is mixing it up with later events like the Civil War.","w":["King John died in 1216, fifty years before this siege, so his supporters were not involved.","","Parliamentarians fought in the Civil War of the 1640s, so this mixes up the centuries.","Dudley owned the castle from the 1560s, about three hundred years after the siege."]}
{"id":"ghi-094","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did Robert Dudley spend so much money on Kenilworth before Elizabeth I's visit in 1575?","o":["To defend it against a Spanish invasion","Because Parliament ordered him to rebuild it","To turn it into a prison for the queen's enemies","To impress the queen and win her favour"],"a":3,"x":"Dudley was one of Elizabeth's favourites and hoped for her favour. He built Leicester's Building and a garden to impress her. The trap is thinking the work was for defence, as most medieval building work was.","w":["The new work was for comfort and show, wrongly assuming all castle building was military.","No order from Parliament made Dudley spend this money; he paid himself to please the queen.","Dudley's building work was for entertaining important guests, not for holding prisoners.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-095","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":2,"q":"After the Civil War, Parliament had Kenilworth's walls breached and its mere drained. Why was this done?","o":["So it could not be used as a stronghold again","Because the Tudors had let it fall into decay","So Henry VIII could close it down","To prepare it for a visit from the king"],"a":0,"x":"This was 'slighting'. Parliament damaged the castle on purpose so that Royalists could not use it again as a military base. The trap is thinking the ruin came from slow neglect rather than deliberate damage.","w":["","The damage was done deliberately around 1650, so blaming slow decay is the common misconception.","Henry VIII died in 1547, a century before the Civil War, so he cannot have ordered it.","Parliament had defeated the king, and slighting made the castle less usable, not ready for royal visits."]}
{"id":"ghi-096","s":"history","t":"hau-2","lv":3,"q":"A historian says Kenilworth shows how castles changed from fortresses to grand homes. Which pair of features best supports this?","o":["The mere and the curtain walls, both built for defence","Leicester's Building and the Elizabethan garden, both for show","The Norman keep for defence and Leicester's Building for show","The ruined walls and the visitor car park, both seen today"],"a":2,"x":"To show change you need one feature from each type of use. The Norman keep was built for defence; the 1570s Leicester's Building, with large windows, was for comfort and show. The trap is picking two features of the same kind.","w":["Both are defensive, so they show no change in use; this is the trap of picking two similar features.","Both are for comfort and show, so they show no move away from defence; change needs a contrast.","","Ruined walls and a car park are modern sights, so they do not show how the castle was used."]}
{"id":"ghi-097","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":1,"q":"What is one advantage of visiting a site such as Kenilworth Castle in person, rather than only reading about it?","o":["You can read the owners' private thoughts in the stonework","You can be sure every part looks as it did when first built","You can judge the size, layout and setting of the buildings","You can find out exactly how many people lived there"],"a":2,"x":"Seeing a site shows scale, layout and setting, which words often cannot. The trap is thinking remains show everything. Later changes and missing parts mean they cannot give exact numbers or thoughts.","w":["Stone shows what was built, not what people thought, so thoughts need written sources.","Parts have been altered, rebuilt or lost, so the remains do not show the original look everywhere.","","Remains cannot give exact numbers of residents, so records are needed, and even those are often incomplete."]}
{"id":"ghi-098","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":2,"q":"Remains of a huge castle kitchen with several big fireplaces and ovens are found. What does this most suggest about everyday life at the castle?","o":["A large household, with servants and guests, had to be fed","Only the lord and his close family ate in the castle","The kitchen was built for show and was never used","Food was normally brought in ready cooked from the town"],"a":0,"x":"Big fireplaces and ovens point to cooking for many people. The trap is ignoring scale. A small family would not need such a kitchen, so the remains hint at a large household and regular visitors.","w":["","A kitchen this size does not fit a small group, so it points to many people being fed.","Several fireplaces and ovens are working features, so they suggest regular cooking rather than display.","Ovens and fireplaces on site show food was cooked there, not brought in from the town."]}
{"id":"ghi-099","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":2,"q":"On a castle wall, historians find a doorway filled in with different stone. Which question about this doorway can the remains help answer best?","o":["Which monarch once slept behind the doorway?","What was the owner's favourite food at the castle?","How many servants used the doorway every day?","When and why was this part of the building changed?"],"a":3,"x":"A blocked doorway shows the building was altered. Differences in stone and style help date the change and suggest reasons. The trap is picking questions about people's lives or habits, which the stonework alone cannot answer.","w":["The filled-in stone shows nothing about who slept there, so this would need written records.","Stonework does not show food preferences, so this cannot be answered from the doorway.","Daily use by servants leaves no countable trace in a blocked doorway, so this cannot be known from it.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-100","s":"history","t":"hau-3","lv":3,"q":"A student says, \"The surviving buildings at Kenilworth show us how everyone there lived.\" What is the best criticism of this claim?","o":["Remains are usually fakes made later, so they cannot be trusted","The grand buildings were made for the owners, so servants' lives are less clear","Written sources never mention the castle, so remains are all we have","Stone buildings only show what happened in a single century"],"a":1,"x":"The best-preserved parts are grand stone buildings made for powerful owners. Ordinary servants and workers left fewer traces. The trap is treating elite remains as typical of everyone, so historians add other sources.","w":["Most remains are genuine, though some parts were rebuilt or lost, so calling them fakes is wrong.","","Many written sources, such as letters and accounts, mention Kenilworth, and this does not answer the student's claim anyway.","Kenilworth has buildings from several periods, so its remains show change over many centuries."]}
{"id":"ghi-101","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":1,"q":"Why did President Jefferson send Lewis and Clark west in 1804?","o":["To drive Native peoples out of the Louisiana Territory","To explore the new land and look for a route to the Pacific","To find gold in California for the government","To mark the border with Spanish Mexico"],"a":1,"x":"Jefferson wanted the land bought from France explored and mapped, and a route to the Pacific found. The trip was about knowledge and trade. The trap is mixing it up with later events like removal or the gold rush.","w":["The expedition was for exploring and mapping. Removal of Native peoples came later, under Jackson.","","Gold in California was found in 1848, decades after the expedition.","The aim was exploring the Louisiana Territory and finding a Pacific route, not marking a border."]}
{"id":"ghi-102","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":2,"q":"What did the Missouri Compromise of 1820 decide?","o":["Missouri joined as a free state and Maine joined as a slave state","Slavery was banned in every new state created in the West","Missouri slave, Maine free, slavery banned elsewhere north of 36°30′","Slavery was allowed in all the land from the Louisiana Purchase"],"a":2,"x":"The Compromise kept a balance between slave and free states. Missouri was slave, Maine was free, and slavery was banned in the rest of the Louisiana land north of 36°30′. The common trap is swapping the states.","w":["The states are the wrong way round: Missouri joined as a slave state and Maine as a free state.","Slavery was not banned everywhere. Missouri was allowed to be a slave state.","","The Compromise banned slavery in the northern part of the Louisiana land, so it was not allowed everywhere."]}
{"id":"ghi-103","s":"history","t":"ma-1","lv":3,"q":"In 1832 the Supreme Court ruled in Worcester v Georgia that Georgia had no power over the Cherokee. What happened next?","o":["Jackson did not enforce the ruling and Cherokee removal went ahead","Jackson sent the army to protect the Cherokee in Georgia","Congress cancelled the Indian Removal Act","The Cherokee were allowed to keep their land in Georgia for ever"],"a":0,"x":"The Court backed the Cherokee, but Jackson did not enforce the ruling. Pressure to remove them continued, and a small group signed the Treaty of New Echota in 1835. The trap is thinking a court ruling meant protection.","w":["","Jackson favoured removal and did not use the army to protect the Cherokee. He ignored the ruling.","Congress did not cancel the 1830 Act. Removal continued despite the ruling.","The ruling did not save their land. Most Cherokee were forced west in 1838-39 on the Trail of Tears."]}
{"id":"ghi-104","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":1,"q":"Where was gold found in January 1848, starting the California Gold Rush?","o":["The Great Salt Lake Valley","Sutter's Mill, on the American River","Independence, Missouri","Fort Laramie, on the Oregon Trail"],"a":1,"x":"James Marshall found gold at John Sutter's sawmill in California in January 1848. The big rush came in 1849, so the migrants were called forty-niners. Do not confuse it with the Mormon site or the trail starting points.","w":["The Great Salt Lake Valley was where the Mormons settled in 1847, not where gold was found.","","Independence, Missouri was a main starting point for the trails west, not a gold site.","Fort Laramie was a trading post on the Oregon Trail, not where gold was found."]}
{"id":"ghi-105","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":2,"q":"Why did Plains Indian ideas about land lead to conflict with white settlers moving west?","o":["They fenced and farmed their land, just as the settlers did","Their chiefs sold land to settlers and then took it back","They believed land could not be owned, but settlers wanted to own and fence it","They demanded that settlers pay rent to use every trail"],"a":2,"x":"Plains peoples saw land as shared and sacred, to be used by the whole group. Settlers saw it as property to buy, fence and farm. This clash of beliefs caused misunderstanding and conflict.","w":["Plains peoples followed the buffalo and did not fence land; this mixes them up with settler farmers.","Plains peoples did not see land as something chiefs could sell; this wrongly gives them settler ideas of ownership.","","Rent for trails was not a Plains Indian belief; this invents a motive instead of the clash over ownership."]}
{"id":"ghi-106","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":2,"q":"The Salt Lake Valley was dry. How did the Mormons manage to farm it after 1847?","o":["They dug irrigation ditches to bring water from mountain streams","They brought food by railway from the eastern states","They bought water from Californian gold miners","They relied on hunting buffalo instead of farming"],"a":0,"x":"Led by Brigham Young, Mormons worked together to dig irrigation channels from mountain streams. Co-operation made the desert farmland productive. The railway did not reach Utah until 1869.","w":["","No railway reached Utah until 1869, so this is the wrong date; the early settlers had to grow their own food.","Gold miners were in California, far away, and did not sell water to the Mormons.","Mormons were farmers and organised crop growing; buffalo hunting was the Plains peoples' way of life."]}
{"id":"ghi-107","s":"history","t":"ma-2","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about the effects of the Gold Rush on California is correct?","o":["Most forty-niners became rich, so few of them stayed on in California","California remained a territory until the 1870s because few people settled there","Native Californians gained from the rush because miners shared land with them","California joined the Union as a state in 1850 after its population grew rapidly"],"a":3,"x":"The rush brought huge numbers of settlers, so California became a state in 1850, only two years after the gold find. Most miners made little money, and Native Californians suffered badly from violence and loss of land.","w":["Most forty-niners made little or no money; the wrong belief that most got rich ignores how hard mining was.","Population soared, so California became a state in 1850, not decades later; this underestimates the rush's scale.","Native Californians were attacked and lost land and lives; this wrongly assumes the rush helped them.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-108","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":1,"q":"What did the 13th Amendment, passed in 1865, do?","o":["It made all African Americans citizens","It abolished slavery across the United States","It gave Black men the right to vote","It ended segregation in schools"],"a":1,"x":"The 13th Amendment banned slavery everywhere in the USA. Citizenship came with the 14th Amendment (1868) and the vote for Black men with the 15th (1870). Do not mix up the three.","w":["Citizenship was granted by the 14th Amendment, not the 13th.","","The vote for Black men came with the 15th Amendment in 1870.","Segregation was not ended by any Reconstruction amendment; it grew later under Jim Crow."]}
{"id":"ghi-109","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":2,"q":"Why did the Emancipation Proclamation of 1 January 1863 not free every enslaved person straight away?","o":["It applied only to rebel areas, not to slave states loyal to the Union","The Supreme Court cancelled it a few weeks after it was issued","It only freed enslaved women and children, not men","It was not due to take effect until the year 1870"],"a":0,"x":"The Proclamation freed enslaved people only in Confederate areas still in rebellion, where Lincoln had no control. Loyal border states were exempt. The limit was in its own wording. Full abolition needed the 13th Amendment in 1865.","w":["","The Supreme Court did not cancel it; the limit was in its own wording.","It applied to enslaved people of all ages in the named areas, not just women and children.","It took effect in 1863; the 1870 date belongs to the 15th Amendment."]}
{"id":"ghi-110","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":2,"q":"What was the main purpose of the Black Codes passed by Southern states in 1865–66?","o":["To give freed people the same rights as white citizens","To set up schools for freed children","To help freed people buy their own land cheaply","To limit freed people's freedom and keep a cheap labour force"],"a":3,"x":"Black Codes used rules such as vagrancy laws and work contracts to control freed people and force them to work for white landowners. Northern anger at them helped the Radical Republicans take control of Reconstruction.","w":["The Codes did the opposite; they restricted rights, which is why Congress reacted.","Schools were mainly run by the Freedmen's Bureau, not by the Black Codes.","The Codes made it harder, not easier, to gain land or independence.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-111","s":"history","t":"ma-3","lv":3,"q":"How did the disputed 1876 presidential election help to end Reconstruction?","o":["Tilden won and ordered the Freedmen's Bureau to close","Hayes became president in a deal and pulled troops out of the South","Hayes sent more federal troops to protect Black voters","Congress cancelled the 14th Amendment after the vote"],"a":1,"x":"In the Compromise of 1877, Republican Hayes took the presidency and federal troops left the South, so white Democrats regained control there. Trap: assuming a Republican president kept protecting Black voters.","w":["Hayes, not Tilden, became president, and the Freedmen's Bureau had already closed in 1872.","","Hayes did the reverse by withdrawing troops, so Southern whites regained control.","The 14th Amendment was never cancelled; it was simply poorly enforced in the South."]}
{"id":"ghi-112","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":1,"q":"Many homesteaders on the Great Plains built their homes from blocks of turf, called sods. What was the main reason for this?","o":["The government banned settlers from cutting down trees","Trees were scarce on the Plains, so timber was hard to get","Turf houses were the only kind that storms could not damage","Settlers copied Plains Indian tipis, which were made of turf"],"a":1,"x":"There were very few trees on the Plains, and timber was costly to bring in. Settlers cut thick blocks of prairie turf instead. Sod houses were cheap but damp and dirty. The trap is thinking they were chosen for strength.","w":["No such ban existed. Settlers used turf because wood was scarce, not because of a law.","","Sod houses could leak and collapse in heavy rain. They were chosen because they were cheap, not storm-proof.","Plains Indian tipis were made from animal hides on wooden poles, not turf."]}
{"id":"ghi-113","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":2,"q":"Why did cow towns such as Abilene in Kansas grow quickly after 1867?","o":["Texan cattle were driven there and loaded onto trains heading east","Gold was found nearby and miners needed beef","The army built forts there and paid ranchers to supply them","Homesteaders there were given free cattle under the Homestead Act"],"a":0,"x":"Cow towns were railheads. Cowboys drove Texan longhorns north along trails, such as the Chisholm Trail. The cattle were sold and put on trains to eastern cities like Chicago. The trap is linking cow towns to mining or army supply.","w":["","The Kansas cow towns grew because of the railroad and cattle trade, not because of a gold rush.","Army forts bought some beef, but the towns grew mainly because of railheads and the cattle trade.","The Homestead Act gave land, not cattle, and it had nothing to do with cow towns."]}
{"id":"ghi-114","s":"history","t":"ma-4","lv":3,"q":"Red Cloud's War ended with the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868. What did the US government agree to in this treaty?","o":["To keep the Bozeman Trail forts open and guard settlers on the trail","To pay the Sioux for the Black Hills, which they agreed to leave","To close the Bozeman Trail forts and recognise the Black Hills as Sioux land","To let the Sioux farm in Oklahoma in return for peace on the Plains"],"a":2,"x":"After Red Cloud's attacks, including the Fetterman defeat in 1866, the army gave up the Bozeman Trail forts. The Black Hills stayed part of the Sioux reservation. The trap is mixing this up with later pressure to sell the Black Hills in 1876-77.","w":["The army abandoned the Bozeman Trail forts; keeping them open was exactly what Red Cloud fought against.","The Sioux did not give up the Black Hills in 1868. This mixes it up with the later seizure after Little Bighorn.","","Moving to Oklahoma was not part of this treaty. The Sioux kept land in Dakota, including the Black Hills."]}
{"id":"ghi-115","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":1,"q":"What was Ellis Island, which opened in 1892?","o":["A reservation in Dakota where Sioux families were moved","A New York harbour station where arriving immigrants were checked","A district of crowded tenements where immigrants lived","A camp built for Chinese workers on the railways"],"a":1,"x":"Ellis Island was the main immigration station in New York harbour. Officials checked the health and papers of new arrivals, mostly from Europe. Do not confuse it with the tenement areas where many then lived.","w":["This mixes up immigration with reservation policy, because Ellis Island was in New York harbour, not on Sioux land in Dakota.","","This confuses where immigrants later lived with the station where they were checked on arrival.","Ellis Island was an immigration station in New York, not a work camp linked to railway building in the West."]}
{"id":"ghi-116","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":1,"q":"Carnegie bought iron ore mines, coal fields and railways that supplied his steel business. What is this called?","o":["Horizontal integration","A trust","Laissez-faire","Vertical integration"],"a":3,"x":"Buying every stage of production, from raw materials to transport, is vertical integration. It cut Carnegie's costs. Horizontal integration is the trap: that means buying up rival firms making the same product.","w":["Horizontal integration means buying rival firms making the same product, but Carnegie bought his suppliers, not his rivals.","A trust was a legal way to run many firms as one, as Rockefeller did, not the name for buying suppliers.","Laissez-faire was the government's policy of not interfering in business, not a description of what Carnegie did.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-117","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":2,"q":"At Carlisle Indian School, pupils had their hair cut, took English names and were banned from speaking their own languages. What was the aim?","o":["To make them give up Native ways and live like white Americans","To help tribes keep their languages while learning new skills","To train them as chiefs who could bargain over treaties","To punish families who had left their reservations"],"a":0,"x":"Boarding schools aimed at assimilation: removing Native culture and teaching white American ways. This matched the thinking behind the Dawes Act. The trap is thinking they protected Native culture, when they tried to end it.","w":["","This is the opposite of the truth, because pupils were punished for using their languages and the schools aimed to remove Native culture.","The schools did not train treaty leaders but taught English, farming and trades to assimilate pupils.","The schools were part of a government assimilation policy, not a punishment for families leaving reservations."]}
{"id":"ghi-118","s":"history","t":"ma-5","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best describes Booker T. Washington's message in his 1895 Atlanta speech?","o":["Black Americans should demand full civil rights and the vote immediately","Black Americans should leave the South and settle in Africa","Black Americans should accept segregation for now and build progress through work and skills","Black Americans should fight back with force against the Ku Klux Klan"],"a":2,"x":"Washington urged Black Americans to focus on education, trades and economic progress and not to challenge segregation for the moment. The trap is confusing him with W.E.B. Du Bois, who demanded equal rights straight away.","w":["This confuses Washington with W.E.B. Du Bois, because Washington was willing to put off demands for political rights.","Washington wanted Black Americans to build their future in the South, not to settle in Africa.","","Washington did not support armed resistance but favoured patience, self-help and cooperation with white leaders."]}
{"id":"ghi-119","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":1,"q":"In May 1933 the Nazis banned independent trade unions. Which Nazi organisation replaced them?","o":["The Hitler Youth","The German Labour Front (DAF)","The Strength Through Joy movement","The SA"],"a":1,"x":"The Nazis seized union buildings on 2 May 1933 and arrested union leaders. The German Labour Front, led by Robert Ley, replaced the unions. It banned strikes. Strength Through Joy came later and was part of it.","w":["The Hitler Youth was for young people, not workers, so it could not replace unions.","","Strength Through Joy was a leisure scheme set up later in 1933 under the DAF; it did not replace unions.","The SA was the Nazi stormtrooper force. It helped attack unions but did not replace them."]}
{"id":"ghi-120","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":2,"q":"Why did Hitler order the killing of Ernst Röhm and other SA leaders in the Night of the Long Knives (30 June 1934)?","o":["The SA had started an armed uprising in Berlin","He wanted to destroy the Communist Party, which was still strong","The SS, led by Röhm, was demanding more power","Army leaders feared the SA, and Hitler wanted the army's support"],"a":3,"x":"Röhm wanted the SA to take over the army. Army leaders were alarmed. Hitler removed the SA threat to win the army's backing. The 'uprising' was an invented excuse. Don't confuse the SA with the SS.","w":["Hitler claimed there was a plot, but no uprising took place; this repeats his false excuse.","The Communist Party had already been crushed in 1933, so this mixes up the earlier events.","Röhm led the SA, not the SS. This confuses the two organisations.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-121","s":"history","t":"nz-1","lv":2,"q":"President Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934. What did Hitler do to strengthen his hold on the army straight afterwards?","o":["He made soldiers swear an oath of loyalty to him personally","He merged the army into the SA","He held a presidential election to let the army choose","He handed control of the army to the Reichstag"],"a":0,"x":"Hitler combined the roles of chancellor and president and called himself Führer. Soldiers then swore loyalty to him personally, not to the constitution. This made it hard for officers to oppose him.","w":["","Hitler had just crushed the SA to please the army, so merging them is the reverse of what happened.","No election took place; Hitler took the presidency's powers himself.","The Reichstag had lost real power by 1934; Hitler took control, not the Reichstag."]}
{"id":"ghi-123","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":1,"q":"Which concentration camp opened near Munich in March 1933 to hold political opponents such as communists?","o":["Dachau","Auschwitz","Buchenwald","Treblinka"],"a":0,"x":"Dachau opened in March 1933, soon after Hitler became Chancellor. It first held communists and socialists. The trap is picking a famous camp: Auschwitz and Treblinka opened later, in occupied Poland, during the war.","w":["","Auschwitz is the best-known camp, but it opened in 1940 in Poland, so the date is wrong.","Buchenwald is a real camp in Germany, but it opened in 1937, not 1933.","Treblinka was a wartime death camp in Poland, opened in 1942, so it is far too late."]}
{"id":"ghi-124","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":1,"q":"Which best describes the Edelweiss Pirates, who opposed the Nazis in the late 1930s?","o":["Middle-class students who loved American jazz and swing music","A Catholic youth group the Nazis let meet in churches","Working-class youths who shunned the Hitler Youth and fought its patrols","A Hitler Youth section set up to spy on disloyal teachers"],"a":2,"x":"The Edelweiss Pirates were mostly working-class teenagers who hated Hitler Youth rules. They hiked, sang their own songs and sometimes fought Hitler Youth patrols. Do not confuse them with the Swing Youth.","w":["This describes the Swing Youth, who were mainly middle-class; mixing up the two youth groups is the slip.","Catholic youth groups were pushed out and later banned, and this is not the Pirates.","","The Pirates opposed the Hitler Youth. They were not part of it and did not spy for it."]}
{"id":"ghi-125","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":2,"q":"In 1933 Hitler signed a Concordat with the Pope. Why did Pope Pius XI write 'Mit brennender Sorge' in 1937?","o":["To tell Catholics to join the Hitler Youth","To protest that the Nazis were breaking the Concordat","To call on Catholics to take up arms against Hitler","To praise Hitler for protecting Catholic schools"],"a":1,"x":"The Concordat promised the Church freedom in return for staying out of politics. The Nazis broke it by attacking Catholic schools and youth groups. The 1937 letter, read in German churches, protested. It did not call for violence.","w":["The Church opposed Nazi pressure on youth groups; the letter did not tell Catholics to join the Hitler Youth.","","The letter was a protest, not a call to armed revolt; assuming opposition always meant violence is the slip.","The letter criticised the Nazis. Catholic schools were being closed, not protected, so this is wrong."]}
{"id":"ghi-126","s":"history","t":"nz-2","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about opposition from the left in Germany in 1933-1934 is correct?","o":["The SPD voted for the Enabling Act, so the Nazis left it alone","The KPD and SPD united to force the Nazis to ban the Hitler Youth","Trade unions were banned in 1939 after a general strike failed","After the Reichstag Fire, KPD leaders were arrested and the SPD banned by mid-1933"],"a":3,"x":"The Reichstag Fire gave the Nazis an excuse to arrest communists. The SPD voted against the Enabling Act but was banned in June 1933. Trade unions were closed in May 1933. The left was also split.","w":["The SPD voted against the Enabling Act and was still banned; confusing it with parties that backed Hitler is the slip.","The two parties never united. Their split weakened them, and the Hitler Youth was not banned.","Trade unions were closed in May 1933 and replaced by the German Labour Front; there was no 1939 general strike.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-127","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":1,"q":"What happened to German trade unions in 1933?","o":["They were allowed to continue but could not call strikes","They were banned and replaced by the German Labour Front (DAF)","They were merged with the Reich Labour Service","They were given control of wages in factories"],"a":1,"x":"In May 1933 the Nazis took over union offices and banned the unions. The DAF replaced them. It was run by the Nazis. Workers lost the right to strike and to bargain for pay.","w":["Unions were abolished, not kept. This option wrongly assumes only the right to strike was lost.","","The Reich Labour Service was a work scheme for young men. It was not a union.","The DAF was run by the Nazis and the state fixed wages. Unions were gone and had no control."]}
{"id":"ghi-128","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":1,"q":"What did the 1933 Law for the Encouragement of Marriage offer?","o":["Free houses for families with four or more children","A higher wage for every married man","Loans to newly married couples, with part cancelled for each child born","Medals for women who gave up work"],"a":2,"x":"The loan was meant to raise the birth rate and push women out of work. Each child cut the amount to be repaid. Medals (the Mother's Cross) were a separate honour for mothers of large families.","w":["Free houses were not offered. This exaggerates the help given to large families.","No wage rise for married men was part of this law. It offered loans.","","Medals were the Mother's Cross, given for having many children, not for leaving work. This mixes up two policies."]}
{"id":"ghi-129","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":2,"q":"By 1938 the Nazis were pushing Jews out of Germany's economic life. What best describes the policy of 'Aryanisation'?","o":["Forcing Jewish owners to sell businesses cheaply to non-Jews","Making all shops show Nazi flags and posters","Taking over Jewish schools to teach race science","Sending Jewish business owners to the Reich Labour Service"],"a":0,"x":"Aryanisation took Jewish-owned firms out of Jewish hands. Owners were pressured to sell far below their value to non-Jews. It was a step in the Nazi plan to remove Jews from economic life.","w":["","Flags and posters were propaganda. This confuses Aryanisation with showing loyalty to the Nazis.","Schools were a different area of persecution. Aryanisation was about businesses and property.","The Reich Labour Service was for young men's work service. It was not used to punish business owners."]}
{"id":"ghi-130","s":"history","t":"nz-3","lv":3,"q":"Official figures show unemployment fell from about 6 million in 1933 to under 1 million in 1939. Why do historians say these figures hide the true picture?","o":["Unemployment benefit was raised, so more people claimed it","Employers hid workers' wages so they paid less tax","Women who left work after marriage loans were counted as unemployed","Jews sacked from jobs, conscripts and Labour Service men were left out"],"a":3,"x":"Rearmament did create real jobs. But the figures left out Jews sacked from work, and conscripts and Labour Service men. Women who left work were also not counted. So the fall looks bigger than it was.","w":["More claimants would push the figures up, not hide unemployment; the real issue is who was left out.","Hidden wages are not the historians' point. The doubt is about who was left out of the count.","Women who left work were not counted as unemployed. Counting them would raise the figures, not hide the true picture.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-131","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":1,"q":"Who were the White Rose group, which opposed the Nazis during the war?","o":["Army officers who planned to kill Hitler with a bomb in 1944","Students at Munich University who spread anti-Nazi leaflets","Working-class teenagers who fought Hitler Youth patrols","Church leaders who spoke out against Nazi policies"],"a":1,"x":"The White Rose was led by students such as Hans and Sophie Scholl. They wrote and handed out leaflets attacking the Nazis. They were caught in 1943 and executed. Do not mix them up with the July Plot officers.","w":["This describes the July Plot of 1944, which was led by army officers and not by students.","","This describes the Edelweiss Pirates, who were working-class teenagers, not university students.","This is a mix-up with church opposition. The White Rose was a student group, not a church one."]}
{"id":"ghi-132","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":2,"q":"Until 1943, the Nazis did not conscript large numbers of women into war work. Which is the best reason?","o":["A law banned women from working in any kind of factory job","Germany had so many men at home that women were not needed","Women's trade unions went on strike against being made to do war work","Nazi ideas put women in the home, and forcing them to work could be unpopular"],"a":3,"x":"Nazi ideology said women should be wives and mothers. The leaders also feared that forcing women into work would lose support. Many women already worked, but compulsory registration of women for war work came only in 1943.","w":["No such ban existed. Many women already worked, so this wrongly assumes they were never in jobs.","Germany was short of workers and relied on forced foreign labour. Men were not plentiful at home.","Trade unions had been abolished in 1933, so there were no union strikes against war work.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-133","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":3,"q":"German armaments output rose sharply in 1942-44, even though Allied bombing increased. Which factor helps to explain this?","o":["Speer streamlined production and used millions of forced foreign workers","Hitler ended rationing so workers had more food and worked harder","Allied bombing of German cities stopped after the Hamburg raids in 1943","Speer moved factories over to consumer goods to keep up morale"],"a":0,"x":"As Armaments Minister from 1942, Speer centralised control, cut the number of designs and made production more efficient. Forced labour from occupied Europe filled the labour gaps. The trap is thinking the bombing stopped or rationing ended.","w":["","Rationing continued and got tighter during the war. It was not ended to boost production.","Bombing increased, with raids on Hamburg and Berlin in 1943 and many more in 1944. It did not stop.","Speer did the reverse. He cut civilian goods production so that factories could make weapons."]}
{"id":"ghi-134","s":"history","t":"nz-4","lv":2,"q":"During the war, what did some Edelweiss Pirates do to oppose the Nazis?","o":["Planted a bomb at Hitler's headquarters","Met at Kreisau to plan a new government after the Nazis","Attacked Hitler Youth patrols and sheltered army deserters","Printed leaflets at Munich University"],"a":2,"x":"The Edelweiss Pirates were mostly working-class teenagers in western cities such as Cologne. Some beat up Hitler Youth members and helped deserters and escapees. The trap is mixing them up with the White Rose or the July Plot.","w":["This was the July Plot of 1944, planned by army officers and not by teenage groups.","This was the Kreisau Circle, a group of educated, upper-class people planning for after the war.","","This was the White Rose, which was a student group, not the Edelweiss Pirates."]}
{"id":"ghi-135","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":1,"q":"Which of these was an extermination camp, built by the Nazis in occupied Poland to murder Jews?","o":["Dachau","Treblinka","Buchenwald","Bergen-Belsen"],"a":1,"x":"Treblinka was an extermination camp in occupied Poland, opened in 1942. Dachau, Buchenwald and Bergen-Belsen were in Germany. The trap is mixing up concentration camps with extermination camps.","w":["Dachau was an early concentration camp in Germany, not an extermination camp in Poland.","","Buchenwald was a concentration camp in Germany, so it is not in occupied Poland.","Bergen-Belsen was a camp in northern Germany, not an extermination camp in Poland."]}
{"id":"ghi-136","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":2,"q":"Why did Nazi rule in occupied Poland become much harsher than in occupied Denmark?","o":["Poland had invaded Germany in 1939, so Hitler wanted revenge","Polish people were the only ones who resisted Nazi rule in Europe","Poland was far from Germany, so it was harder to control","Nazi ideas saw Slavs as inferior and wanted their land for Germans"],"a":3,"x":"Nazi race ideas called Slavs 'Untermenschen' and the Nazis wanted Lebensraum in the east. So Poles faced murder, forced labour and land seizure. Denmark was seen as more 'Aryan' and was treated more lightly at first.","w":["Germany invaded Poland in September 1939, not the other way round; the Nazis faked a Polish attack.","Resistance happened in many countries, and harsh rule in Poland came from race ideas, not just resistance.","Poland borders Germany, so distance is wrong. The cause was race ideology and Lebensraum.",""]}
{"id":"ghi-137","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":2,"q":"What happened to most Danish Jews in October 1943, when the Germans planned to arrest them?","o":["Many Danes helped to ship them by boat to neutral Sweden","Danish officials helped the Gestapo to arrest almost all of them","The Danish army fought German troops to protect them","Britain flew them out of Denmark in planes"],"a":0,"x":"After a warning, ordinary Danes, including fishermen, helped about 7,000 Jews cross to neutral Sweden by boat. Denmark is an example of rescue and resistance, not of collaboration in the Holocaust.","w":["","Danish officials did not hand over Jews, so this confuses Denmark with other occupied countries.","The Germans had disarmed the Danish army in August 1943, so it could not fight to protect them.","The escape was by sea to Sweden, not by British aircraft; this invents a rescue."]}
{"id":"ghi-138","s":"history","t":"nz-5","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about the Warsaw Ghetto Uprising of April 1943 is correct?","o":["The Polish Home Army freed the city of Warsaw from German rule","Allied paratroopers landed to help the fighters in the ghetto","Poorly armed Jewish fighters held off the SS for weeks before defeat","It began when Soviet troops reached Warsaw and the Germans fled"],"a":2,"x":"When the SS came to deport the last people in the ghetto, Jewish fighters with few weapons held out for about four weeks. The ghetto was destroyed. The trap is confusing it with the separate Warsaw Uprising of 1944.","w":["This mixes it up with the 1944 Warsaw Uprising, which also failed to free the city.","No Allied troops landed in Warsaw; the fighters were mostly alone with very few weapons.","","The Soviets did not arrive until 1944–45. The uprising began when the SS returned to deport people."]}
